cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "gfckit: a general-fractional-calculus toolkit for degradation identifiability"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Conrard Giresse"
    family-names: "Tetsassi Feugmo"
    email: giresse.feugmo@gmail.com
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-09"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Feugmo-Group/gfckit"
abstract: >-
  gfckit identifies the memory structure of a degradation process, such as
  battery capacity fade or corrosion film growth, from a measured aging curve,
  and maps how much of that structure a given measurement can actually
  recover. It models the observable with a general fractional evolution
  equation whose kernel is a general Sonin kernel rather than a fixed power
  law, and recovers the effective anomalous order, a tempered pair, or a full
  order spectrum depending on what the data support. Recovery is validated
  against independent mechanistic ground truth (a moving-boundary
  solid-electrolyte-interphase model and a Point Defect Model of passive-film
  growth, both solved by Jacobian-free Newton-Krylov) and against a
  closed-form analytic benchmark (de Levie's porous electrode). A separate
  module attributes the dominant degradation mechanism behind a simulated or
  real battery cell from its capacity fade, differential-voltage, and
  impedance signatures using a leave-one-configuration-out k-nearest-neighbour
  classifier. All numerical code is pure JAX.
keywords:
  - fractional calculus
  - general fractional calculus
  - anomalous diffusion
  - degradation modeling
  - battery aging
  - corrosion
  - identifiability
  - inverse problems
  - jax
